WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.967
A classic rag workflow might retrieve a return policy that says standard returns are accepted within fourteen days of

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:13.162
delivery. Then the language model generates an answer from that policy.

00:00:13.162 --> 00:00:17.137
That may sound reasonable, but notice what is missing.

00:00:17.137 --> 00:00:21.250
The workflow may never verify the actual order record.

00:00:21.851 --> 00:00:27.283
If the user says the order was delivered twenty days ago, should we trust that value?

00:00:27.283 --> 00:00:29.708
In some applications, maybe.

00:00:29.708 --> 00:00:36.378
In a real support system, the authoritative delivery date should probably come from the order database.

00:00:36.378 --> 00:00:39.623
This is a useful lesson for project design.

00:00:39.623 --> 00:00:46.816
Retrieval can give you relevant general knowledge, but some questions also require exact structured facts.

00:00:47.395 --> 00:00:51.987
A strong system asks not only, did I retrieve relevant text?

00:00:51.987 --> 00:00:57.427
It also asks, do I have every fact needed to answer this specific case?
